00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:14.060
key pain points that you have
around network performance

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:16.550
monitoring and diagnostics,
especially when you

00:05:16.550 --> 00:05:19.790
look at your infrastructure or
applications running in cloud?

00:05:19.790 --> 00:05:23.333
And maybe if you can give some
examples of some issues that

00:05:23.333 --> 00:05:25.625
have happened or some outages
that might have happened.

00:05:25.625 --> 00:05:28.087
And Ed, maybe I'll
start with you here?

00:05:28.087 --> 00:05:30.170
ED HAMMOND: Sure, I've got
the mic, so I'll start.

00:05:32.810 --> 00:05:36.470
Well, one of the problems
is we have a large staff

00:05:36.470 --> 00:05:38.780
and things are moving
very, very quickly.

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:41.480
Things are changing
all the time.

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:43.010
I'll give you a case in point.

00:05:43.010 --> 00:05:45.470
Recently, a very
skilled network engineer

00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:48.440
was trying to work on
a particular problem

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:52.350
between shared VPCs
and that kind of thing.

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:56.470
And he had totally forgotten
that we had multiple other VPCs

00:05:56.470 --> 00:05:59.030
in a post static route
in one of the VPCs

00:05:59.030 --> 00:06:02.990
which propagated to on prem
routers and took out routes.

00:06:02.990 --> 00:06:05.060
And that wasn't very nice.

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:06.500
That was one that was--

00:06:06.500 --> 00:06:09.080
if he had just stepped
back and actually looked

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:11.160
at the configuration
a little bit better,

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:12.540
he would've found it.

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:14.540
He knew it was there but
had forgotten about it.

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:18.595
It didn't scream at him before
he put in that configuration.

00:06:18.595 --> 00:06:19.970
There was another
one that we had

00:06:19.970 --> 00:06:23.900
where we had a database
release, application

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:25.820
release that we were
moving into the cloud.

00:06:25.820 --> 00:06:28.220
And they put the
database in one VPC

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:30.470
and they put the
application in another VPC.

00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:33.150
That's not very good
for performance.

00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:36.660
So we were exiting Iowa
and going up to Chicago

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:39.150
and back to Iowa for every
single database call.

00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:41.600
That added significant latency.

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:44.840
So again, in that case,
it was on the console,

00:06:44.840 --> 00:06:48.830
and everybody looked
at it, including me.

00:06:48.830 --> 00:06:49.790
And we didn't see it.

00:06:49.790 --> 00:06:52.520
It wasn't intuitively
obvious to us.

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:57.643
So if we had a clear methodology
there, that would help us.

00:06:57.643 --> 00:06:59.310
SHISHIR AGRAWAL:
That's a great example.

00:06:59.310 --> 00:06:59.913
Thank you.

00:06:59.913 --> 00:07:00.830
KELLY WANG: Thank you.

00:07:00.830 --> 00:07:03.500
So the challenge we're
facing is that we

00:07:03.500 --> 00:07:06.020
have a different workload.

00:07:06.020 --> 00:07:08.930
We have different
requirements which

00:07:08.930 --> 00:07:11.260
requires some of
the workload we're

00:07:11.260 --> 00:07:13.130
putting in the shared VPC.

00:07:13.130 --> 00:07:16.190
Some, they are in their own VPC.

00:07:16.190 --> 00:07:20.180
So the challenge
we're facing is we

00:07:20.180 --> 00:07:24.010
want to know at any single
point how many VPCs we own.

00:07:24.010 --> 00:07:30.350
Within a particular VPCs, how
many projects are associate,

00:07:30.350 --> 00:07:33.200
how many public IPs we own.

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:38.360
And within each this, if
we change firewall rules,

00:07:38.360 --> 00:07:41.540
how many VMs will get impact.

00:07:41.540 --> 00:07:44.480
So currently the
challenges we're facing

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:50.060
is that there are missing
a single pane of view

00:07:50.060 --> 00:07:54.020
from a network
monitoring perspective.

00:07:54.020 --> 00:07:57.380
And those are one of the
challenge we're facing.

00:07:57.380 --> 00:07:59.690
The other things
we're facing is if we

00:07:59.690 --> 00:08:05.150
want to do some
connectivity diagnosis,

00:08:05.150 --> 00:08:08.720
we want to know point
to point what are the--

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:11.270
is there a connectivity
between them,

00:08:11.270 --> 00:08:13.580
and what's the-- if
there are, what what's

00:08:13.580 --> 00:08:15.380
the latencies between them?

00:08:15.380 --> 00:08:19.200
If there is no connectivity,
what might be the issue?

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:22.920
So those are the challenges
we're facing today.

00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:24.680
SHISHIR AGRAWAL:
Thank you, Kelly.

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:26.840
For Justin, I mean we
heard from our customers,

00:08:26.840 --> 00:08:29.900
but as a partner, I'm sure you
talk to a lot of customers.

00:08:29.900 --> 00:08:33.140
So you can you share
your views on what do you

00:08:33.140 --> 00:08:33.973
hear from customers?

00:08:33.973 --> 00:08:35.307
JUSTIN RYBURN: Yeah, absolutely.

00:08:35.307 --> 00:08:37.039
I think when we
talk to customers

00:08:37.039 --> 00:08:40.101
about the performance monitoring
diagnostics challenges

00:08:40.101 --> 00:08:42.559
that they have in their network
and in their infrastructure

00:08:42.559 --> 00:08:44.142
in Google Cloud, it
usually comes down

00:08:44.142 --> 00:08:45.470
a lot of call three buckets.

00:08:45.470 --> 00:08:48.810
And I think Ed and Kelly
hit on a number of those--

00:08:48.810 --> 00:08:50.060
it's cost.

00:08:50.060 --> 00:08:53.000
So using as example of
traffic exiting one VPC

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:55.260
and coming back into
another, performance

00:08:55.260 --> 00:08:56.177
is one aspect of that.

00:08:56.177 --> 00:08:57.635
But there's a cost
to that as well.

00:08:57.635 --> 00:08:59.210
So if you optimize
your applications

00:08:59.210 --> 00:09:01.190
and you have the
applications database running

00:09:01.190 --> 00:09:05.210
in the same VPC, you avoid cost
of that traffic hair-pinning,

00:09:05.210 --> 00:09:07.037
if you want to
phrase it that way.

00:09:07.037 --> 00:09:08.995
The second one is
performance, as Ed mentioned,

00:09:08.995 --> 00:09:12.290
and in the third bucket that
we hear a lot from customers,

00:09:12.290 --> 00:09:13.760
is security.

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:17.660
How do I know that the policies
and the security posture that I

00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:20.385
think I have both in
my physical data center

00:09:20.385 --> 00:09:22.510
as well as in my Cloud are
actually being enforced?

00:09:22.510 --> 00:09:24.385
Is traffic getting
through that shouldn't be?

00:09:24.385 --> 00:09:26.150
And am I unintentionally
blocking traffic

00:09:26.150 --> 00:09:29.330
that I otherwise
need to allow through

00:09:29.330 --> 00:09:31.880
as part of my as part of
my security infrastructure?

00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:35.570
Because configuring
ACLs in the VPCs

00:09:35.570 --> 00:09:38.657
can be fairly complex as
your environment scales out.

00:09:38.657 --> 00:09:40.240
So those are kind
of the three buckets

00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:43.730
of challenges that we hear from
customers when we talk to them.

00:09:43.730 --> 00:09:45.350
SHISHIR AGRAWAL: Thanks Justin.

00:09:45.350 --> 00:09:47.788
So Gargi, I mean, as I
mentioned at the beginning,

00:09:47.788 --> 00:09:49.580
we have actually talked
a lot of customers.

00:09:49.580 --> 00:09:52.035
And a lot of these
themes we have heard--

00:09:52.035 --> 00:09:54.410
would you like to elaborate
a bit on maybe something else

00:09:54.410 --> 00:09:56.510
that we have heard
from customers?

00:09:56.510 --> 00:09:57.830
GARGI ADHAV: Yeah, sure.

00:09:57.830 --> 00:10:00.500
So when Ed was
talking and Justin

00:10:00.500 --> 00:10:02.810
as well about the
performance thing, that

00:10:02.810 --> 00:10:06.840
is like a resounding thing that
we've heard from customers,

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:09.830
especially when their
performance deteriorates

00:10:09.830 --> 00:10:11.450
that they would--
they today have

00:10:11.450 --> 00:10:13.940
no way of knowing why
is that deteriorating,

00:10:13.940 --> 00:10:16.950
where is the
bottleneck, and so on.

00:10:16.950 --> 00:10:18.920
So there has been
a resounding thing.

00:10:18.920 --> 00:10:23.330
The other part of it is
the configuration changes.

00:10:23.330 --> 00:10:25.850
And sometimes if it
is a syntax error,

00:10:25.850 --> 00:10:28.610
then yes obviously GCP
will throw an error

00:10:28.610 --> 00:10:30.690
and you will find
out immediately.

00:10:30.690 --> 00:10:33.140
But syntactically, your
configuration could be correct.

00:10:33.140 --> 00:10:36.290
It could be accepted by the
VMs and everything as fine,

00:10:36.290 --> 00:10:39.680
but then suddenly there will
be some impact on your traffic

00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:41.300
a few minutes later.

00:10:41.300 --> 00:10:44.030
And sometimes they don't
even have visibility

00:10:44.030 --> 00:10:47.030
because they do have multiple
network admins and security

00:10:47.030 --> 00:10:49.130
admins who are both
changing things.

00:10:49.130 --> 00:10:51.450
So maybe nothing changed in
the networking conflicts.

00:10:51.450 --> 00:10:54.590
But the security admin came
and added an extra firewall

00:10:54.590 --> 00:10:55.440
rule or something.

00:10:55.440 --> 00:10:59.510
And then it goes all the
way up to troubleshooting

00:10:59.510 --> 00:11:02.940
your network, and then the
app admins come and say, hey,

00:11:02.940 --> 00:11:07.430
my service is not available
to this XYZ customer.

00:11:07.430 --> 00:11:10.250
And go and see it's
a network problem.

00:11:10.250 --> 00:11:13.550
So figuring out what the root
cause and the pain point is

00:11:13.550 --> 00:11:16.040
and where exactly
something went wrong

00:11:16.040 --> 00:11:19.378
is a sounding thing we've
heard from many of you.

00:11:19.378 --> 00:11:20.670
SHISHIR AGRAWAL: Thanks, Gargi.

00:11:20.670 --> 00:11:22.450
So I guess, I mean, if I
were to just summarize this,

00:11:22.450 --> 00:11:24.460
I think we heard a
bit about security's

00:11:24.460 --> 00:11:25.480
obviously top of mind.

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:27.480
How do you make sure you
have the right security

00:11:27.480 --> 00:11:29.920
posture and firewall rules?

00:11:29.920 --> 00:11:32.230
Kelly talked about
configuration changes

00:11:32.230 --> 00:11:37.040
and how do you make sure any
kind of connectivity issues,

00:11:37.040 --> 00:11:39.310
especially in that
hybrid environment,

00:11:39.310 --> 00:11:42.520
you can actually make sure
that any of the configuration

00:11:42.520 --> 00:11:44.810
changes would not cause
that connectivity issue.

00:11:44.810 --> 00:11:48.460
And I'd actually had
a very good example

00:11:48.460 --> 00:11:50.230
of somebody cleaning
their static routes

00:11:50.230 --> 00:11:53.120
and causing connectivity
to be broken.

00:11:53.120 --> 00:11:56.440
So just by show of
hand, I mean what

00:11:56.440 --> 00:12:01.720
percentage of network outages
and performance issues

00:12:01.720 --> 00:12:04.480
happened because of
configuration changes?

00:12:04.480 --> 00:12:07.585
Anybody want to take a guess
at the percentage here?

00:12:07.585 --> 00:12:08.800
AUDIENCE: 80%.

00:12:08.800 --> 00:12:10.580
SHISHIR AGRAWAL: 80%.

00:12:10.580 --> 00:12:11.740
Anybody else?

00:12:11.740 --> 00:12:12.620
AUDIENCE: 60%.

00:12:12.620 --> 00:12:13.515
SHISHIR AGRAWAL: 60%.

00:12:13.515 --> 00:12:14.640
Actually that's very close.

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:16.790
I mean the data is--

00:12:16.790 --> 00:12:20.390
the data shows that about
75% of network outages

00:12:20.390 --> 00:12:23.050
and configuration happen
because of configuration related

00:12:23.050 --> 00:12:23.550
changes--

00:12:23.550 --> 00:12:25.092
AUDIENCE: You could
say I was closer.

00:12:25.092 --> 00:12:26.435
SHISHIR AGRAWAL: Yeah.

00:12:26.435 --> 00:12:28.820
One of you are closer.

00:12:28.820 --> 00:12:30.980
So again, I think
the point being

00:12:30.980 --> 00:12:33.740
that hopefully most
of you in the room

00:12:33.740 --> 00:12:36.530
are related to the
network team and you

00:12:36.530 --> 00:12:43.200
can realize that configuration
does cost a lot of outages.

00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:47.540
So moving on to the next
part, maybe the next question

00:12:47.540 --> 00:12:50.102
I would like to ask the
panelists is about some

00:12:50.102 --> 00:12:51.560
of the tools and
solutions they are

00:12:51.560 --> 00:12:55.370
using to address
these challenges

00:12:55.370 --> 00:12:57.830
and do monitoring and
diagnostics-- whether it's

00:12:57.830 --> 00:13:01.130
homegrown, whether it's
Google-provided solutions,

00:13:01.130 --> 00:13:02.380
or whether it's a third party.

00:13:02.380 --> 00:13:05.020
So maybe, Kelly, I would
like to start with you here

00:13:05.020 --> 00:13:07.533
and if you want to talk
about some solutions you use?

00:13:07.533 --> 00:13:08.450
KELLY WANG: All right.

00:13:08.450 --> 00:13:11.040
So today we are--

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:14.810
have stacks of some
solutions and tools

00:13:14.810 --> 00:13:19.490
together to help us with
monitoring and diagnosis.

00:13:19.490 --> 00:13:21.620
From the platform
perspective, we

00:13:21.620 --> 00:13:24.620
are-- turn on all
the VPC Flow Logs,

00:13:24.620 --> 00:13:29.490
which give us the network
performance information.

00:13:29.490 --> 00:13:33.860
They also provide
security posture for us.

00:13:33.860 --> 00:13:40.490
So we know from a particular IP
what's a resource he's touching

00:13:40.490 --> 00:13:42.080
and at what time.

00:13:42.080 --> 00:13:45.980
So and those are at
the VPC Flow Log level.

00:13:45.980 --> 00:13:50.160
We also turn on the
firewall rule logs as well.

00:13:50.160 --> 00:13:54.350
So those give us the firewall
rules changing information,

00:13:54.350 --> 00:13:56.960
and what are the allowed--

00:13:56.960 --> 00:14:00.110
what are the denied
firewall rules?

00:14:00.110 --> 00:14:04.350
Other than the
platform-provided capabilities,

00:14:04.350 --> 00:14:09.620
we also rely on the traditional
layer 2, layer 3 tools

00:14:09.620 --> 00:14:11.690
as well like Trace Route.

00:14:11.690 --> 00:14:16.070
And also on top of
that, we also want

00:14:16.070 --> 00:14:19.850
to get like network
topology information.

00:14:19.850 --> 00:14:23.480
And those are we are currently
introduce a third party too.

00:14:28.090 --> 00:14:32.150
ED HAMMOND: We too use
firewall logs very heavily.

00:14:32.150 --> 00:14:34.560
I'll give you an example.

00:14:34.560 --> 00:14:37.610
We have instances that
are running on premise

00:14:37.610 --> 00:14:39.207
and we bring them
into the cloud.

00:14:39.207 --> 00:14:40.790
As we bring them
into the cloud, we're

00:14:40.790 --> 00:14:43.100
trying to do micro segmentation.

00:14:43.100 --> 00:14:46.850
So we're using a
particular firewall rule

00:14:46.850 --> 00:14:48.960
that we apply to
all these instances.

00:14:48.960 --> 00:14:51.105
And then we look at the
log traffic of that,

00:14:51.105 --> 00:14:52.730
and then we can lock
down the instances

00:14:52.730 --> 00:14:55.370
with the specific
firewall rule logs.

00:14:55.370 --> 00:14:57.800
That turned out to be a
significant amount of effort

00:14:57.800 --> 00:14:59.960
that we had to develop
these tools about,

00:14:59.960 --> 00:15:03.080
so having intelligent
firewall log

00:15:03.080 --> 00:15:05.480
management would
be very helpful,

00:15:05.480 --> 00:15:08.840
as well as explaining that
to application developers who

00:15:08.840 --> 00:15:12.060
don't necessarily understand
how all their applications work.

00:15:12.060 --> 00:15:16.040
I'm sure our developers
are unique in that regard.

00:15:16.040 --> 00:15:18.800
As well as the performance--

00:15:18.800 --> 00:15:20.240
Kelly mentioned the performance.

00:15:20.240 --> 00:15:23.240
So we've actually installed
third party tools,

00:15:23.240 --> 00:15:25.760
where at different
points in our network

00:15:25.760 --> 00:15:27.500
we have instrumentation points.

00:15:27.500 --> 00:15:29.450
And so we can tell
response times

00:15:29.450 --> 00:15:31.490
between different segments.

00:15:31.490 --> 00:15:35.660
And that turns out to be really
sensitive to our applications--

00:15:35.660 --> 00:15:40.060
2 or 3 milliseconds seems to
be very sensitive at times.

00:15:40.060 --> 00:15:42.170
SHISHIR AGRAWAL: OK.

00:15:42.170 --> 00:15:43.530
Thank you.

00:15:43.530 --> 00:15:45.380
So apparently, I
mean you are-- both

00:15:45.380 --> 00:15:48.380
of you are using VPC firewall
logs and VPC Flow Logs.

00:15:48.380 --> 00:15:52.430
And again, just quick poll among
the audience-- how many of you

00:15:52.430 --> 00:15:54.830
are using the VPC Flow
Logs and Firewall Logs

00:15:54.830 --> 00:15:58.248
for monitoring or diagnostics?

00:15:58.248 --> 00:15:59.040
So not a whole lot.

00:15:59.040 --> 00:16:01.590
So again, in the later
part of the presentation,

00:16:01.590 --> 00:16:04.950
I'll actually talk a bit
more about VPC Flow Logs

00:16:04.950 --> 00:16:05.700
and firewall logs.

00:16:05.700 --> 00:16:08.790
But that is actually--
we see that a lot

00:16:08.790 --> 00:16:12.360
among our customers using VPC
Flow Logs and firewall logs.

00:16:12.360 --> 00:16:20.050
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:16:20.050 --> 00:16:21.800
ED HAMMOND: Why not
contact me afterwards?

00:16:21.800 --> 00:16:23.817
KELLY WANG: Yeah,
that's my answer too.

00:16:23.817 --> 00:16:26.150
SHISHIR AGRAWAL: Yeah, and
I'll actually talk about some

00:16:26.150 --> 00:16:28.790
of the partner ecosystem we
have in a subsequent slide.

00:16:28.790 --> 00:16:30.770
But definitely we are--

00:16:30.770 --> 00:16:32.210
again with Google
we are obviously

00:16:32.210 --> 00:16:36.800
providing you code capabilities
as part of the platform.

00:16:36.800 --> 00:16:38.300
But we have an open
ecosystem where

00:16:38.300 --> 00:16:41.330
we have a lot of partners
that integrate with us

00:16:41.330 --> 00:16:43.140
via the logs or the APIs.

00:16:43.140 --> 00:16:45.550
So a lot of customers do that.

00:16:48.690 --> 00:16:52.180
OK, so the next question
I would like to ask

00:16:52.180 --> 00:16:55.810
is, if you had a
magic wand and if you

00:16:55.810 --> 00:16:59.740
were to wish, I would say,
one or two key capabilities

00:16:59.740 --> 00:17:05.530
that GCP would provide you
that would make the life of you

00:17:05.530 --> 00:17:09.819
as a network admin operator
enterprise architect easier,

00:17:09.819 --> 00:17:13.922
what would those one
or two things would be?

00:17:13.922 --> 00:17:15.550
And Kelly, maybe I would--

00:17:15.550 --> 00:17:16.569
KELLY WANG: I'll start.

00:17:16.569 --> 00:17:23.440
So I want to have a
platform provide me

00:17:23.440 --> 00:17:26.920
a view which this will--

00:17:26.920 --> 00:17:31.240
even the operator, the operation
team, they don't have--

00:17:31.240 --> 00:17:34.870
they're not be a very
skilled network engineer.

00:17:34.870 --> 00:17:37.600
They can come into
this tool and the

00:17:37.600 --> 00:17:42.280
are from this
platform-provided view there,

00:17:42.280 --> 00:17:45.880
they can find the
topology information.

00:17:45.880 --> 00:17:49.600
So they can see, what
are the VPCs we own.

00:17:49.600 --> 00:17:53.200
Within each VPC, what's
my topology views?

00:17:53.200 --> 00:18:00.790
And inside this VPC,
per subnet, how many

00:18:00.790 --> 00:18:02.800
VMs are associated with?

00:18:02.800 --> 00:18:08.110
How many public IPs are
associated with a VPC?

00:18:08.110 --> 00:18:11.920
How many public IPs are
associated under our account.

00:18:11.920 --> 00:18:18.340
And also I wish we have
this view capabilities.

00:18:18.340 --> 00:18:21.330
We can monitoring the network
performance inside of--

00:18:21.330 --> 00:18:24.310
I go into the VPC Flow Logs.

00:18:24.310 --> 00:18:26.530
We have that information
in the flow log,

00:18:26.530 --> 00:18:31.380
but it is very time consuming
to get that information today.

00:18:31.380 --> 00:18:33.220
So if we have this
view, that would

00:18:33.220 --> 00:18:37.880
be ideal for operation team
in case there's incidents

00:18:37.880 --> 00:18:40.490
happening, they can
do the first phase

00:18:40.490 --> 00:18:46.360
triad without involving
a whole bigger team.

00:18:46.360 --> 00:18:53.980
And also if this tool provided
diagnostic capability,

00:18:53.980 --> 00:18:57.130
so they can provide
point-to-point connectivity

00:18:57.130 --> 00:19:02.200
information and tell
what the latencies are,

00:19:02.200 --> 00:19:08.190
and those will be the things
I think of my top wish list.

00:19:08.190 --> 00:19:09.670
SHISHIR AGRAWAL:
OK, thanks Kelly.

00:19:09.670 --> 00:19:11.350
Ed?

00:19:11.350 --> 00:19:14.330
ED HAMMOND: I have to echo
a lot what Kelly said.

00:19:14.330 --> 00:19:17.140
One of the other things
about performance

00:19:17.140 --> 00:19:20.530
is that you can
use it as a canary

00:19:20.530 --> 00:19:23.540
as to whether
something's going wrong.

00:19:23.540 --> 00:19:25.780
For example, if I
know that I normally

00:19:25.780 --> 00:19:29.230
have traffic going from US
central to, I don't know,

00:19:29.230 --> 00:19:32.740
Europe or whatever, and it's
supposed to take so much time,

00:19:32.740 --> 00:19:35.110
and then all of a sudden
it starts inching up,

00:19:35.110 --> 00:19:38.590
I know that I'm doing
something somewhere that

00:19:38.590 --> 00:19:40.710
could indicate a failure.

00:19:40.710 --> 00:19:43.330
Now, we always want the
network to be highly resilient.

00:19:43.330 --> 00:19:47.500
But I want to be able to
detect that and alarm--

00:19:47.500 --> 00:19:50.970
not necessarily alarm
but give trend analysis,

00:19:50.970 --> 00:19:54.440
so that I can understand when
something has gone wrong.

00:19:54.440 --> 00:19:56.930
So sometimes those are
very, very subtle signals.

00:19:56.930 --> 00:19:58.967
And if you don't look
at it all the time--

00:19:58.967 --> 00:20:01.300
I don't about you, but I don't
look at all these reports

00:20:01.300 --> 00:20:02.288
all the time--

00:20:02.288 --> 00:20:03.830
if you don't look
at it all the time,

00:20:03.830 --> 00:20:05.410
you could miss these things.

00:20:05.410 --> 00:20:08.710
Was it 1.2 seconds or
was it 1.7 seconds?

00:20:08.710 --> 00:20:10.150
I don't remember.

00:20:10.150 --> 00:20:13.510
And so having a
tool that would be

00:20:13.510 --> 00:20:16.240
very sensitive in that regard.

00:20:16.240 --> 00:20:18.617
The other one is in
that firewall rules--

00:20:18.617 --> 00:20:20.200
when somebody's going
to put something

00:20:20.200 --> 00:20:23.860
in knowing what the effect
is before it happens

00:20:23.860 --> 00:20:26.050
would be a really
good thing to have

00:20:26.050 --> 00:20:28.300
before I go change a
static route, knowing

00:20:28.300 --> 00:20:32.800
before it happens that I'm
going to have a BGP propagation

00:20:32.800 --> 00:20:33.850
problem.

00:20:33.850 --> 00:20:35.990
That would be really,
really nice to know.

00:20:35.990 --> 00:20:39.160
So I think with a
lot of capabilities

00:20:39.160 --> 00:20:42.220
that Google can do,
they're in a great position

00:20:42.220 --> 00:20:43.400
to help us with that.

00:20:43.400 --> 00:20:44.650
SHISHIR AGRAWAL: Great, great.

00:20:44.650 --> 00:20:46.030
Thank you.

00:20:46.030 --> 00:20:48.710
So I think what I heard
about just to summarize some

00:20:48.710 --> 00:20:50.810
of the things, I
think Kelly mentioned

00:20:50.810 --> 00:20:54.020
about getting a
topology view, maybe

00:20:54.020 --> 00:20:56.030
getting better
performance metrics right

00:20:56.030 --> 00:20:58.877
out the out of the box as
opposed to mining a lot of data

00:20:58.877 --> 00:21:00.210
and mining a lot of information.

00:21:00.210 --> 00:21:03.520
And I think Ed's
wish is how they

00:21:03.520 --> 00:21:06.230
can manage configuration
changes, which

00:21:06.230 --> 00:21:08.880
is even before things happen.

00:21:08.880 --> 00:21:11.890
So and I know Gargi here
has the magic wand, I think.

00:21:11.890 --> 00:21:16.590
So Gargi, do you want to
address some of what we

00:21:16.590 --> 00:21:17.840
have heard from the customers?

00:21:17.840 --> 00:21:20.480
And maybe talk a
bit about at least

00:21:20.480 --> 00:21:22.100
how Google is
looking at addressing

00:21:22.100 --> 00:21:23.810
some of these problems?

00:21:23.810 --> 00:21:24.860
GARGI ADHAV: Sure, sure.

00:21:24.860 --> 00:21:27.600
So obviously, like
Shishir mentioned,

00:21:27.600 --> 00:21:30.290
we have VPC logs
and VPC Flow Logs,

00:21:30.290 --> 00:21:34.490
Firewall Logs that help you
troubleshoot your problems

00:21:34.490 --> 00:21:37.880
and help us troubleshoot
if those logs are enabled.

00:21:37.880 --> 00:21:42.290
But it looks like not many of
you have been using it so far.

00:21:42.290 --> 00:21:45.110
But some of our partners,
including some here,

00:21:45.110 --> 00:21:48.500
they also shared that they
use-- have those logs,

00:21:48.500 --> 00:21:51.680
and then they write scripts
on top of those logs to find--

00:21:51.680 --> 00:21:56.960
do fine-tune and identify where
is the needle in the haystack.

00:21:56.960 --> 00:21:59.480
But one of the things
that we've been hearing

00:21:59.480 --> 00:22:02.690
is, like finding the
needle in the haystack,

00:22:02.690 --> 00:22:04.360
whether Google can
help you with that.

00:22:04.360 --> 00:22:06.110
And that's exactly
what we're going to do.

00:22:06.110 --> 00:22:09.440
Because there is a lot of
information, like Kelly said.

00:22:09.440 --> 00:22:12.260
It's hidden and it's
hard to go and navigate

00:22:12.260 --> 00:22:14.420
because you don't even
anticipate that, oh,

00:22:14.420 --> 00:22:18.410
this thing under this tree would
have some information in there.

00:22:18.410 --> 00:22:21.510
So that is definitely
something that can helps it.

00:22:21.510 --> 00:22:23.490
But one thing I'd like
to ask all of you,

00:22:23.490 --> 00:22:26.750
