00:24:35.880 --> 00:24:37.990
So I want to actually
kind of change gears

00:24:37.990 --> 00:24:41.950
a little bit and talk about what
are the existing solutions that

00:24:41.950 --> 00:24:44.230
are available today that
a lot of our customers

00:24:44.230 --> 00:24:47.290
use, and actually you can
go back and use as well.

00:24:47.290 --> 00:24:51.790
So one of the key capabilities
that we have is VPC Flow Logs.

00:24:51.790 --> 00:24:53.680
And again, the idea
here is that we

00:24:53.680 --> 00:24:56.410
record a sample of
the network flows

00:24:56.410 --> 00:25:00.170
that are sent or received
by the VM instances.

00:25:00.170 --> 00:25:02.240
Those flows are aggregated
every 5 seconds.

00:25:02.240 --> 00:25:04.050
And actually with
the new capability

00:25:04.050 --> 00:25:05.800
that's currently in
beta, you can actually

00:25:05.800 --> 00:25:08.340
configure that integral.

00:25:08.340 --> 00:25:12.050
A lot of our customers
use VPC Flow Logs

00:25:12.050 --> 00:25:13.448
for a variety of use cases.

00:25:13.448 --> 00:25:15.490
The first one is obviously
in network monitoring,

00:25:15.490 --> 00:25:17.860
where they can look
at the flow data,

00:25:17.860 --> 00:25:20.090
look at kind of who are
the top talkers, where

00:25:20.090 --> 00:25:25.060
is the traffic coming from, even
use that data for optimizing

00:25:25.060 --> 00:25:27.460
their expenses or network.

00:25:27.460 --> 00:25:29.560
Security again, a
lot of customers pipe

00:25:29.560 --> 00:25:32.080
VPC Flow Logs to
maybe a sim solution

00:25:32.080 --> 00:25:35.110
and look at whether the
connections are happening

00:25:35.110 --> 00:25:37.800
from legit IP addresses or not.

00:25:37.800 --> 00:25:41.200
So then they can apply the
right kind of policies.

00:25:41.200 --> 00:25:43.520
You can view these VPC flow
logs in the stack driver,

00:25:43.520 --> 00:25:48.310
and then also you can export
it to Cloud Pub/Sub or BigQuery

00:25:48.310 --> 00:25:51.660
or a third party
solution like Sims.

00:25:51.660 --> 00:25:55.580
And one of the key capabilities
that actually a lot of you who

00:25:55.580 --> 00:25:57.570
might have used VPC
Flow Logs have asked us

00:25:57.570 --> 00:26:01.310
around how can you reduce
the volume of VPC Flow Logs--

00:26:01.310 --> 00:26:04.490
so again we have some new
controls available now, which

00:26:04.490 --> 00:26:08.280
is in beta, to actually allow
you to reduce the VPC Flow Logs

00:26:08.280 --> 00:26:08.780
volume.

00:26:08.780 --> 00:26:12.690
So you can control the
time interval in which

00:26:12.690 --> 00:26:13.950
the aggregation happens.

00:26:13.950 --> 00:26:15.890
So by default, it happens
every five seconds,

00:26:15.890 --> 00:26:20.480
but now you can set it to 30
seconds, a minute, 10 minutes,

00:26:20.480 --> 00:26:22.520
or 15 minutes.

00:26:22.520 --> 00:26:26.450
Similarly for log entry
sampling, so we sample--

00:26:26.450 --> 00:26:28.900
so when we write the
logs to the database,

00:26:28.900 --> 00:26:30.320
there is a sampling
that happens.

00:26:30.320 --> 00:26:35.640
And by default, the sampling
ratio is 0.5 or 50%,

00:26:35.640 --> 00:26:39.320
but now actually can control
it you can set it to 100%

00:26:39.320 --> 00:26:41.650
or on the extreme and
you can set it to 0%.

00:26:41.650 --> 00:26:43.150
So that's another
control let's have

00:26:43.150 --> 00:26:45.920
a label along with
metadata annotations

00:26:45.920 --> 00:26:48.740
where the notion is that
when we write VPC Flow Logs,

00:26:48.740 --> 00:26:53.000
we actually write the source and
destination VM instance names.

00:26:53.000 --> 00:26:56.780
We write the
geographical information

00:26:56.780 --> 00:26:58.800
in terms of regions.

00:26:58.800 --> 00:27:02.840
So all of that you can actually
choose to not annotate your VPC

00:27:02.840 --> 00:27:06.350
Flow Logs, because your use case
might be simply to know the IP

00:27:06.350 --> 00:27:07.310
addresses.

00:27:07.310 --> 00:27:10.600
So those are the controls
that are available to you.

00:27:10.600 --> 00:27:14.120
And the other key
capabilities is Firewall Logs.

00:27:14.120 --> 00:27:18.220
So this allows you to actually
audit, verify, and analyze

00:27:18.220 --> 00:27:20.170
the effects of your
firewall rules.

00:27:20.170 --> 00:27:23.500
So you can actually
understand if a firewall rule

00:27:23.500 --> 00:27:27.170
that you have, it is doing
what is intended to do.

00:27:27.170 --> 00:27:29.020
So for example, if it's
blocking the traffic

00:27:29.020 --> 00:27:31.090
that you want it to block.

00:27:31.090 --> 00:27:33.130
So you enable these
firewall rules,

00:27:33.130 --> 00:27:35.140
logging on a per rule basis.

00:27:35.140 --> 00:27:38.837
And unlike VPC Flow Logs,
these are not samples.

00:27:38.837 --> 00:27:40.670
So these are available
for every connection.

00:27:40.670 --> 00:27:42.790
And you can see them in
a Stackdriver logging

00:27:42.790 --> 00:27:45.700
or exported to a pub
server or BigQuery.

00:27:48.400 --> 00:27:52.660
In addition to logging
within the Cloud console,

00:27:52.660 --> 00:27:55.610
we also have very, very rich
monitoring capabilities.

00:27:55.610 --> 00:27:58.270
So on the service pages, and
I am taking an example of VPN

00:27:58.270 --> 00:27:58.970
here.

00:27:58.970 --> 00:28:00.762
If you go to the VPN
page, you can actually

00:28:00.762 --> 00:28:04.930
see some key information for
monitoring out of the box.

00:28:04.930 --> 00:28:07.810
You can see what is
the VPN general status.

00:28:07.810 --> 00:28:10.180
If it's green, as in
if it's established,

00:28:10.180 --> 00:28:12.430
or it might be red,
which means that it's not

00:28:12.430 --> 00:28:14.042
seeing any incoming packets.

00:28:14.042 --> 00:28:16.000
This is some basic level
of monitoring that you

00:28:16.000 --> 00:28:18.380
see on the service pages.

00:28:18.380 --> 00:28:20.990
And similarly you will
see for interconnect.

00:28:20.990 --> 00:28:23.530
But then we publish
a lot of metrics

00:28:23.530 --> 00:28:25.270
that you can see
in the Stackdriver.

00:28:25.270 --> 00:28:28.450
So Stackdriver has out
of the box dashboards.

00:28:28.450 --> 00:28:31.900
For example, if you go to
resources under Stackdriver,

00:28:31.900 --> 00:28:34.900
under Services VPN,
we actually are now

00:28:34.900 --> 00:28:37.390
publishing metrics such
as terminal status,

00:28:37.390 --> 00:28:40.360
received bytes, packet loss--

00:28:40.360 --> 00:28:42.760
which is dropped packets,
which is one of the key metrics

00:28:42.760 --> 00:28:44.570
that customers want to look at.

00:28:44.570 --> 00:28:49.140
And then you can set
alerts on these metrics.

00:28:49.140 --> 00:28:50.890
And depending on the
thresholds you want.

00:28:50.890 --> 00:28:53.600
So you can say, if my
packet drop is less than--

00:28:53.600 --> 00:28:57.490
or is more than whatever point,
0.1%, I want to get an alert.

00:28:57.490 --> 00:29:00.700
But these dashboards are
available out of the box.

00:29:03.210 --> 00:29:05.640
And last, but again
very important,

00:29:05.640 --> 00:29:07.450
we have a rich
partner ecosystem.

00:29:07.450 --> 00:29:10.170
So to some of the question that
somebody was asking earlier,

00:29:10.170 --> 00:29:13.410
we have integrations with Sim
partners for VPC Flow Logs

00:29:13.410 --> 00:29:14.350
and Firewall Logs.

00:29:14.350 --> 00:29:19.420
So you can send data to similar
Sumo Logic or Splunk solutions.

00:29:19.420 --> 00:29:21.870
A lot of customers use
next generation firewall,

00:29:21.870 --> 00:29:24.055
such as Palo Alto
or Fortinet or Check

00:29:24.055 --> 00:29:27.000
Point where, again,
they're sitting in line

00:29:27.000 --> 00:29:30.150
and it has full visibility
of the traffic that's

00:29:30.150 --> 00:29:31.650
flowing through your network.

00:29:31.650 --> 00:29:35.810
And for compliance
reasons, we have, again,

00:29:35.810 --> 00:29:40.290
a lot of vendors or partners who
integrate with VPC Flow Logs.

00:29:40.290 --> 00:29:43.810
And again, you'll hear from
one of the partners, Kentik,

00:29:43.810 --> 00:29:44.760
in a minute.

00:29:44.760 --> 00:29:48.160
But that's another
integration that's available.

00:29:48.160 --> 00:29:50.670
So with that, I think
it's a good segue

00:29:50.670 --> 00:29:52.320
into the last
section here, where

00:29:52.320 --> 00:29:55.260
I would invite Justin
to talk about Kentik

00:29:55.260 --> 00:29:56.850
and show us a quick demo.

00:30:02.977 --> 00:30:04.810
JUSTIN RYBURN: All
right, thank you Shishir.

00:30:04.810 --> 00:30:06.685
So for those who aren't
familiar with Kentik,

00:30:06.685 --> 00:30:10.450
I'll be brief on this, but
just kind of a quick overview

00:30:10.450 --> 00:30:11.410
about who Kentik is.

00:30:11.410 --> 00:30:13.420
We're about a five-year-old
startup that's

00:30:13.420 --> 00:30:17.090
based here in San Francisco.

00:30:17.090 --> 00:30:19.840
We've seen good success
over the last five years

00:30:19.840 --> 00:30:24.340
across what we call digital
enterprise as well as

00:30:24.340 --> 00:30:25.750
the service provider community.

00:30:28.760 --> 00:30:30.650
This is how our
platform is built.

00:30:30.650 --> 00:30:34.610
So as Shishir mentioned,
that the Google VPC Flow

00:30:34.610 --> 00:30:38.180
Logs can be exported
to a Pub/Sub bus

00:30:38.180 --> 00:30:42.020
and then basically you can
figure your VPCs to send

00:30:42.020 --> 00:30:47.450
those logs to that bus and
then you configure our system

00:30:47.450 --> 00:30:51.080
to listen, to subscribe
to that conversation,

00:30:51.080 --> 00:30:54.780
pull those flow logs
back into our system.

00:30:54.780 --> 00:30:56.300
And then we enrich
that with a lot

00:30:56.300 --> 00:30:58.640
of different pieces of context.

00:30:58.640 --> 00:31:00.937
So Flow Logs out of
the box from Google

00:31:00.937 --> 00:31:02.395
give you a lot of
good information,

00:31:02.395 --> 00:31:06.030
as Shishir mentioned,
around what's the VM source?

00:31:06.030 --> 00:31:09.230
What's the zone source
What's the project source?

00:31:09.230 --> 00:31:12.600
There's a lot of good, rich
information that's in there.

00:31:12.600 --> 00:31:17.180
But we also have some additional
metadata that will enrich with

00:31:17.180 --> 00:31:20.060
as well things from
Kubernetes or Istio

00:31:20.060 --> 00:31:23.720
if you're running those
type of service mesh setups.

00:31:23.720 --> 00:31:26.750
We can find out a little bit
more information about pods

00:31:26.750 --> 00:31:29.098
and nodes and so forth.

00:31:29.098 --> 00:31:31.140
And then once we have
those logs into our system,

00:31:31.140 --> 00:31:34.760
I'll give you some examples of
what some of the UI screenshots

00:31:34.760 --> 00:31:35.965
would look like.

00:31:35.965 --> 00:31:37.340
We give you all
kinds of insights

00:31:37.340 --> 00:31:41.090
about what your traffic
looks like in both our UI

00:31:41.090 --> 00:31:43.100
is as well as give
you an API that's

00:31:43.100 --> 00:31:45.770
available to pull that data
out if you want to integrate it

00:31:45.770 --> 00:31:47.240
with another system.

00:31:47.240 --> 00:31:50.510
And then all kinds of proactive
notifications and alerts

00:31:50.510 --> 00:31:52.880
that you can use to
find out proactively

00:31:52.880 --> 00:31:56.395
about changes in your network
environment, in your VPCs.

00:31:58.945 --> 00:32:00.320
So what are some
of the use cases

00:32:00.320 --> 00:32:02.720
and why would people use Kentik?

00:32:02.720 --> 00:32:06.200
And a lot of it comes down
to the persona of the user.

00:32:06.200 --> 00:32:09.290
So for our network operations
and our network engineering

00:32:09.290 --> 00:32:13.187
teams, kind of to the point
that Gargi made earlier,

00:32:13.187 --> 00:32:15.020
the age old question--
is it the application

00:32:15.020 --> 00:32:15.950
or is it the network?

00:32:15.950 --> 00:32:21.440
And so using Kentik and
looking at the flow logs

00:32:21.440 --> 00:32:25.010
and finding out, am I seeing
an increase in latency

00:32:25.010 --> 00:32:27.530
for just a single application,
all the applications

00:32:27.530 --> 00:32:30.950
in a single VPC, can give me
some clues and some indicators

00:32:30.950 --> 00:32:33.295
into, is my network
performance-- is it

00:32:33.295 --> 00:32:35.420
a network performance issue
or is it an application

00:32:35.420 --> 00:32:36.170
performance issue?

00:32:36.170 --> 00:32:39.260
Which is really powerful to our
network operations, our network

00:32:39.260 --> 00:32:40.772
engineering users.

00:32:40.772 --> 00:32:42.995
On the sec ops
side, it comes back

00:32:42.995 --> 00:32:45.890
to I think what I was mentioning
earlier about security.

00:32:45.890 --> 00:32:48.140
Is the security
posture that I believe

00:32:48.140 --> 00:32:50.480
I have enforced
actually working the way

00:32:50.480 --> 00:32:53.770
that I believe it is once my
configuration gets applied?

00:32:53.770 --> 00:32:55.820
And I allowing things
through that shouldn't be?

00:32:55.820 --> 00:33:00.110
Am I blocking things that
I mean to allow through?

00:33:00.110 --> 00:33:01.910
On the SRE and dev
ops side, there's

00:33:01.910 --> 00:33:04.790
a lot of trying to figure out
what applications are talking

00:33:04.790 --> 00:33:05.920
to what other applications.

00:33:05.920 --> 00:33:07.587
And I mentioned, we
have a rich API that

00:33:07.587 --> 00:33:10.460
allows you to pull some of
this data out, which allows you

00:33:10.460 --> 00:33:12.890
to automate a lot
of your systems

00:33:12.890 --> 00:33:15.920
to do some automation
built on top of changes

00:33:15.920 --> 00:33:18.650
to network traffic patterns
or certain traffic patterns

00:33:18.650 --> 00:33:20.030
that you see in
your environment.

00:33:20.030 --> 00:33:21.530
And then for the
executives, we have

00:33:21.530 --> 00:33:24.410
sort of a higher level dashboard
so they can get a view of,

00:33:24.410 --> 00:33:25.830
how many VPC do I have?

00:33:25.830 --> 00:33:27.970
How much traffic is
coming in and out of them?

00:33:27.970 --> 00:33:31.282
How is my cloud
network performing?

00:33:34.660 --> 00:33:36.640
Just some screenshots
as examples

00:33:36.640 --> 00:33:39.140
that might be interesting to
network engineering and network

00:33:39.140 --> 00:33:40.107
operations.

00:33:40.107 --> 00:33:41.690
This is what we call
a Sankey diagram,

00:33:41.690 --> 00:33:44.770
and this shows coming out
of one particular project,

00:33:44.770 --> 00:33:46.220
it's spread across US--

00:33:46.220 --> 00:33:48.500
well, US East and US West.

00:33:48.500 --> 00:33:51.290
What are the destination IP
addresses that that traffic is

00:33:51.290 --> 00:33:52.408
flowing to?

00:33:52.408 --> 00:33:54.450
And what is the application
this particular case?

00:33:54.450 --> 00:33:57.780
Of course, it's a
secure web application.

00:33:57.780 --> 00:34:00.830
So as a network engineering
or network operations user,

00:34:00.830 --> 00:34:05.357
I can quickly see how my how
my network is performing.

00:34:05.357 --> 00:34:07.190
For security operations,
again, like I said,

00:34:07.190 --> 00:34:09.370
it's really about what--

00:34:09.370 --> 00:34:13.210
are their data exfiltration
events going on?

00:34:13.210 --> 00:34:15.340
Is my security policy
being applied the way

00:34:15.340 --> 00:34:16.991
that I think it's being applied?

00:34:16.991 --> 00:34:18.699
This is where the
proactive notifications

00:34:18.699 --> 00:34:21.670
come in really useful, is I can
get notifications when there's

00:34:21.670 --> 00:34:23.830
a new event that takes
place in the network,

00:34:23.830 --> 00:34:26.560
and then drill down and get
a lot more detail about what

00:34:26.560 --> 00:34:27.699
that network event is.

00:34:31.500 --> 00:34:33.580
Latency-- so this
is something that

00:34:33.580 --> 00:34:35.820
is a really powerful
feature that Google enables

00:34:35.820 --> 00:34:39.420
by putting latency information
in each one of the flow

00:34:39.420 --> 00:34:40.650
logs that they export.

00:34:40.650 --> 00:34:42.690
Now I can look at,
on a per application

00:34:42.690 --> 00:34:45.000
basis, what is my latency?

00:34:45.000 --> 00:34:48.130
And am I seeing an increase in
the latency of my application.

00:34:48.130 --> 00:34:50.130
So in this particular
example here on the slide,

00:34:50.130 --> 00:34:53.010
you see just one
application out of many

00:34:53.010 --> 00:34:55.020
has a huge spike in its latency.

00:34:55.020 --> 00:34:57.960
So that gives me an
indicator that likely this

00:34:57.960 --> 00:35:01.740
is a network-- or an application
latency issue and not a network

00:35:01.740 --> 00:35:02.540
issue.

00:35:02.540 --> 00:35:05.160
And so I can try and defend
myself as a networking guy

00:35:05.160 --> 00:35:07.230
and say no, it's
not the VPC network,

00:35:07.230 --> 00:35:11.530
it's something to do
with your application.

00:35:11.530 --> 00:35:14.180
And then like I said, for the
executives, a dashboard that

00:35:14.180 --> 00:35:15.980
shows a lot of
different views of,

00:35:15.980 --> 00:35:18.170
how is the network
performing at a high level?

00:35:18.170 --> 00:35:19.310
How much traffic do I have?

00:35:19.310 --> 00:35:20.310
Where is it coming from?

00:35:20.310 --> 00:35:21.900
Where is it going to?

00:35:21.900 --> 00:35:25.410
And how much is it costing me?

00:35:25.410 --> 00:35:29.770
So as an example, more
concrete, we here at Kentik

00:35:29.770 --> 00:35:31.660
have an application
we call the Sock

00:35:31.660 --> 00:35:33.090
Shop that's running on GCPs.

00:35:33.090 --> 00:35:35.450
So this is an example workload.

00:35:35.450 --> 00:35:38.830
It's a microservices
containerized application

00:35:38.830 --> 00:35:40.330
that has various pieces to it.

00:35:40.330 --> 00:35:43.300
It has a card, it has a catalog.

00:35:43.300 --> 00:35:48.750
And so as a user that runs
this e-commerce website,

00:35:48.750 --> 00:35:50.750
there are a couple of
things that I can look at.

00:35:50.750 --> 00:35:52.125
So the first thing
that I look at

00:35:52.125 --> 00:35:56.380
is a dashboard that
shows me all of my VPC

00:35:56.380 --> 00:35:58.150
traffic for this application.

00:35:58.150 --> 00:36:03.070
It shows me my traffic zone
to zone, region to region,

00:36:03.070 --> 00:36:04.742
project to project.

00:36:04.742 --> 00:36:06.700
So I can kind of see how
are the various pieces

00:36:06.700 --> 00:36:08.330
of my application running.

00:36:08.330 --> 00:36:10.695
And to Ed's point earlier,
I can try and look forward,

00:36:10.695 --> 00:36:12.070
do I have
inefficiencies in where

00:36:12.070 --> 00:36:13.320
my workloads have been placed?

00:36:13.320 --> 00:36:16.600
Do I have the cart
or the catalog

00:36:16.600 --> 00:36:18.280
running in a different
VPC than what

00:36:18.280 --> 00:36:20.350
I have my database running in?

00:36:20.350 --> 00:36:25.840
And is that something that
I could better optimize?

00:36:25.840 --> 00:36:28.510
And then this is
another view that

00:36:28.510 --> 00:36:32.260
gives us some information about
this microservices application

00:36:32.260 --> 00:36:33.250
that's running.

00:36:33.250 --> 00:36:36.475
It's showing me the various
pieces of the application

00:36:36.475 --> 00:36:37.600
and what's talking to what.

00:36:37.600 --> 00:36:40.400
So my front end
website, for example,

00:36:40.400 --> 00:36:43.450
talking back to the
Sock Shop, the catalog

00:36:43.450 --> 00:36:44.735
talking to the cart.

00:36:44.735 --> 00:36:46.360
There's a lot of
different interactions

00:36:46.360 --> 00:36:48.430
that are happening
in that application.

00:36:48.430 --> 00:36:52.360
And I can see how each one
of those maps to each other,

00:36:52.360 --> 00:36:55.120
whether that be bits per
second, whether that be

00:36:55.120 --> 00:36:58.490
packets per second, or
even latency information.

00:36:58.490 --> 00:37:01.030
I can drill down and get
a lot more information

00:37:01.030 --> 00:37:03.040
about how the
application is performing

00:37:03.040 --> 00:37:05.678
and look for
inefficiencies in how

00:37:05.678 --> 00:37:06.970
that application is performing.

00:37:06.970 --> 00:37:10.320
[MUSIC PLAYING]

